{"author":"rebar-bridge[bot]","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"compacted_at":1793168325759683114,"compiled_state":{"alias":"refreshing-cosmic-jaeger","assignee":null,"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","authorship":{"signed":3,"unsigned":0},"authorship_ledger":[{"content_hash":"bed8538d5027e06ecfeb5ede27d31c7129b195e7146e20ee490318c4b7715e0c","event_uuid":"89edb252-705f-4cbd-b0b5-f051fb9b37e8","position":{"commit_sha":"d5bbb0bcad2869516b2aedaba174b49c8684254a","position":"1793158599954089388-89edb252-705f-4cbd-b0b5-f051fb9b37e8"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null},{"content_hash":"cb372ac20460e290ca902ae0fcaad69802efe9c92b479f07e58850c76e3e9fbd","event_uuid":"1bd94c56-a8f1-49f8-9254-92135bb5b623","position":{"commit_sha":"d0f8a1bdc55e07b5bf9904ebe74d6e3de598d7d5","position":"1793158609999994955-1bd94c56-a8f1-49f8-9254-92135bb5b623"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null},{"content_hash":"5c72ec22418ae80760bf3b1867efc6dc179be23a35879786edb125d49522e525","event_uuid":"fe641dd1-5113-4e7b-918e-e716dc906c33","position":{"commit_sha":"cd99fc29c97fedbf0651e05c87d97acd2c22cba5","position":"1793163629943206889-fe641dd1-5113-4e7b-918e-e716dc906c33"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"## The recorded diagnosis is refuted. The de-dup key already carries the material fingerprint.\n\nInvestigated 2026-10-27 ~21:50–22:05 PDT, before changing anything.\n\n### Three pieces of counter-evidence\n\n1. **The reproduction shows no de-dup happened.** Step 2 returned job `…dbe7ada2` and step 4 returned job `…41c67599`. A de-duplicated start *attaches to the existing job and returns its id*. Two different job ids mean the singleflight key did **not** match, so the start was not swallowed.\n2. **The key already includes the fingerprint.** `_mcp_llm._plan_review_variant` is\n   `f\"source={source or 'attested'};material={current_material_fingerprint(ticket_id)}\"`, used at both the `review_plan_start` and sync `review_plan` call sites. It was added by commit `864b6f56c91` (\"Key plan-review dedup on current plan material\", 2026-10-18, ticket `3b27-8f4b-90bd-44b2`), which is an ancestor of `origin/main`. Its own commit message describes precisely the failure this ticket reports, and it shipped nine days before this observation.\n3. **`stale-material` is not a de-dup artifact.** The only producers are `llm/plan_review/attest.py` (lines 667 and 718), inside attestation *validation*: it compares the ticket's **recorded** attestation's signed material fingerprint against the current one and returns `{\"valid\": False, \"verdict\": \"stale-material\"}`. That is why the returned `signed_at` was step 2's timestamp — it is the step-2 attestation being reported as stale, not a replayed run.\n\nSo \"the start path's de-dup key does not include the plan material fingerprint\" is false as written, and a change to that key would have fixed nothing.\n\n### What remains unexplained\n\nWhy the step-4 job produced **no findings** (`plan_reviews: 0`) and surfaced the stale-attestation verdict instead of running a fresh review, while `force=True` on the identical tree produced a real certified PASS. Two candidate mechanisms, neither yet distinguished:\n\n1. A **reuse short-circuit** fired. `llm/plan_review/__init__.py` calls `reuse.idempotent_reuse` then `reuse.verdict_reuse`, both bypassed by `force` — which matches the observed force/no-force asymmetry exactly. But on inspection neither should fire here: `idempotent_reuse` returns `None` when `claim_gate_check` is not ok (a stale attestation is not ok), and `verdict_reuse` requires the stored sidecar's `material_fingerprint` to still match. Both guards read as correct, so if one fired, the bug is in its guard, not in the key.\n2. A **precheck ahead of the LLM** returned the validation result directly. That would explain the empty findings and the replayed `signed_at` without any reuse path firing.\n\nDistinguishing these needs an instrumented run, not more reading — the two paths produce the same observable payload. I did not run one: a plan review is a billable 15–20 minute LLM operation and I could not justify spending one on a hypothesis I could not first narrow statically.\n\n### `verify_completion_start` audit (acceptance criterion 3)\n\n**It does not share the refuted plan-review defect, but its key is weaker.** Its variant at both call sites is `f\"graph={graph};source={source or 'attested'}\"` — no material fingerprint at all. The completion-verifier attestation *does* bind material (`attest.py` line 667 validates it the same way), so a completion start issued after a material edit de-duplicates onto a run that read the earlier material. That is the defect this ticket's title describes, present in `verify_completion_start` rather than in `review_plan_start`.\n\n### Recommendation\n\nRetitle and rescope around the two findings above: (a) `verify_completion_start`'s dedup variant should carry the material fingerprint, mirroring `_plan_review_variant`; (b) the plan-review symptom needs one instrumented reproduction to choose between the reuse-guard and precheck mechanisms before any code changes. Acceptance criterion 1 as written — \"does not de-dup onto a job started before that change\" — is already satisfied by `864b6f5` and should be replaced by one that names the actual observed failure: a non-forced re-review after a material edit must produce its own findings rather than reporting the superseded attestation.\n\nNo code changed for this ticket in this session.","timestamp":1793163629943206889}],"created_at":1793158599954089388,"creation_channel":"mcp","deps":[{"link_uuid":"1bd94c56-a8f1-49f8-9254-92135bb5b623","relation":"discovered_from","target_id":"6b66-9378-ebb1-446c"}],"description":"## Symptom\n\n`review_plan_start` returned a **stale BLOCK** for a plan whose material had just been widened, instead of reviewing the new material. The returned `durable` block was the attestation from the *previous* run.\n\nObserved on story `6b66-9378-ebb1-446c`:\n\n1. `set_file_impact` → 13 paths.\n2. `review_plan_start` → job `…dbe7ada2`, `verdict: PASS`, `signed_at: 1793154820371400435`.\n3. `set_file_impact` → **14** paths (added `tests/unit/workflow/test_contracts.py`).\n4. `review_plan_start` → job `…41c67599`, **`verdict: BLOCK`**, `durable.verdict: \"stale-material\"`, `reason: \"the plan was materially edited since review — changed: file_impact (13 -> 14 paths)\"`, and `signed_at: 1793154820371400435` — **the timestamp from step 2**.\n5. `show_ticket` reported `plan_reviews: 0` — no findings were produced for the step-4 job.\n6. `review_plan_start(force=True)` → job `…702b44ed`, `verdict: PASS`, `durable.verdict: certified`, `signed_at: 1793158405677634370`. Same tree, same SHA `ee5253abfd4`.\n\nThe only difference between steps 4 and 6 is `force=True`.\n\n## Diagnosis\n\nThe start path's de-dup key does not include the plan material fingerprint. Step 4 matched the step-2 job on ticket + basis, attached to it, and surfaced its already-computed result — which, re-evaluated against the now-changed material, renders as `stale-material`. No fresh review ran, which is why `plan_reviews` stayed empty.\n\nThe tool's own contract says \"Duplicate ticket/basis starts share a job\". The defect is that **a material edit does not make a start non-duplicate**, even though a material edit is precisely what invalidates the prior result.\n\n## Why this is worth fixing rather than documenting\n\nThe failure is silent and inverted. An agent that widens `file_impact` — which AGENTS.md actively requires when a change touches a file the plan did not declare — and then re-reviews gets a BLOCK verdict carrying a plausible, *accurate-sounding* reason (\"the plan was materially edited since review\"). The natural reading is \"my plan is blocked, go revise it\". The correct reading is \"your start was swallowed; re-run with force\". Nothing in the response distinguishes those.\n\nWorse, `gate_status` reported `status: \"passed\"` and `findings.readable: true` with a `current-review-result-sidecar` for the step-4 job, so the surfaces that an agent would use to sanity-check disagree with each other.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A `review_plan_start` issued after the ticket's reviewed material changed (description or `file_impact`) does **not** de-dup onto a job started before that change; it runs a fresh review.\n- [ ] Regression test: start a review, mutate `file_impact`, start again without `force`, and assert the second job produces its own findings and a certified attestation rather than replaying the first job's verdict.\n- [ ] `verify_completion_start` is audited for the same de-dup key defect and fixed or shown to be unaffected.\n- [ ] If de-dup across a material change is ever intentional, the response must say so explicitly rather than surfacing a bare `stale-material` that reads as a plan defect.\n\nDiscovered while implementing story `6b66-9378-ebb1-446c`; cost one confused round trip.","env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","file_impact":[],"file_impact_scope":"undeclared","keyring":[],"managed_refs":[],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"","plan_review_phase":"planning","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":2,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"open","tags":["mcp","plan-review-gate","correctness"],"ticket_id":"dc9b-e792-bad3-4578","ticket_type":"bug","title":"review_plan_start de-dups across a material change and replays the prior run's stale verdict","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["89edb252-705f-4cbd-b0b5-f051fb9b37e8","1bd94c56-a8f1-49f8-9254-92135bb5b623","fe641dd1-5113-4e7b-918e-e716dc906c33"]},"env_id":"","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1793168325759683114,"uuid":"a524df92-b03f-45df-b99e-455291693b55"}